          "quote": "IBM reserves the right to investigate potential violations of the representations and warranties in Subsection 11.2(b). If IBM reasonably determines that a breach of any such warranty has occurred, then IBM may, in its sole and reasonable discretion:",
          "rationale": "The Agreement grants IBM no audit, inspection, verification or usage-examination right against Bluefly: searches for \"audit\", \"inspect\", \"verify\", \"records\", \"monitor\" and \"premises\" return nothing relevant, and no such right appears in §§ 4 (Charges/Payment), 11 (IBM obligations/Suspected Violations), 12 (License) or 13-14. The closest hook, § 11.3, is only a right for IBM to investigate suspected breaches of the Customer content/acceptable-use warranties and to restrict access, remove Content or terminate, coupled with an attempt-to-notify commitment (p0137); it confers no access to Bluefly's systems, personnel, books or records and imposes no audit cost or cooperation burden on us. Because there is no audit right binding the Customer, the position is better than the preferred position (annual, 30 days' notice, independent auditor, Vendor cost, no system access), so nothing needs to be inserted or limited — adding an audit framework would only create a right IBM does not currently have. Residual note (not a deviation): § 11.3's investigation right is uncapped in frequency and the notice undertaking is best-efforts only, but as it is a breach-remedy provision without any access to our environment we would leave it as drafted.",
